# captainhook.json — what each action does

CaptainHook is the Git-hooks manager CodeGuard installs by default
(ADR-010, replacing Lefthook). JSON does not support inline comments,
so this adjacent README documents each action. Edit both files in
sync: if you change the config, update the explanation here.

- Docs: https://php.captainhook.info
- Run manually: `vendor/bin/captainhook hook:pre-commit` (or any hook)
- Disable temporarily: `CAPTAINHOOK_SKIP=1 git commit ...`

## Where the installed hook scripts actually live

Run `git config --get core.hooksPath` in your project. If it prints
a path (typical for repos migrating from Husky, which sets
`core.hooksPath=.husky/_`), Git will invoke hooks from THERE — not
from `.git/hooks/`. CaptainHook honours the setting: `vendor/bin/
captainhook install` will write its hook shims into whichever path
Git is pointing at. This is expected behaviour; the hooks still run
on every commit/push.

To confirm which path hosts the active hooks on your machine:

```
git config --get core.hooksPath   # empty → .git/hooks ; else → that path
ls -la "$(git config --get core.hooksPath || echo .git/hooks)/pre-commit"
```

If you want Git to use the default `.git/hooks/` again, clear the
override with `git config --unset core.hooksPath` and re-run
`vendor/bin/captainhook install --force --only-enabled`.

## commit-msg

Empty by default. Add actions here for:
- Conventional Commits enforcement (`\\CaptainHook\\App\\Hook\\Message\\Action\\Regex` or similar)
- Jira/Linear issue-key requirement
- Signed-off-by check (`SignedOff`)

## pre-commit

Runs on every `git commit`, against the files you staged.

### pint --dirty

Laravel Pint (PHP-CS-Fixer wrapper with Laravel preset). `--dirty`
limits formatting to the files Git already considers modified, so
the hook is fast even in large repos. If Pint rewrites a file,
you'll need to `git add` it again before re-running the commit
(CaptainHook has no built-in equivalent of Lefthook's `stage_fixed:
true` yet — tracked as a follow-up PHP Action).

Condition `FileStaged\\OfType(php)` skips this action when no PHP
files are staged (for example, a commit that only touches README).

### StagedPhpFilesRunner (PHPStan)

This is a PHP Action shipped by CodeGuard. It reads the currently
staged `*.php` files from Git and passes ONLY those paths to
`vendor/bin/phpstan analyse` — matching what Lefthook's
`{staged_files}` template used to do.

Without this runner, a plain `vendor/bin/phpstan analyse` shell
action would re-scan the entire `paths:` block in `phpstan.neon`
on every commit (minutes on large codebases). With it, pre-commit
stays in the seconds range.

Options you can tune inline:
- `binary`: which analyser to run (default `vendor/bin/phpstan`)
- `flags`: prefix flags passed before the staged file list. Use
  this to change memory limit, configuration file, or to switch
  to `--level=max`.

Same `FileStaged\\OfType(php)` condition applies — no staged PHP,
no invocation.

## pre-push

Runs on `git push`, before anything hits the remote. This is where
the heavier gates live so the commit loop stays fast.

### pest --bail

Full Pest test suite with `--bail` (stop on first failure). If any
test fails, the push is aborted — push logs show the failing test
and you fix it locally before re-pushing.

If your suite is slow enough that pre-push friction becomes a
problem, consider:
- Running only unit tests here: `vendor/bin/pest --testsuite=Unit --bail`
- Moving the full suite to CI and keeping pre-push focused on the
  fast tier.

## Extending

The power of CaptainHook (vs a shell-only runner) is PHP Actions.
CodeGuard's own `StagedPhpFilesRunner` is a tiny example — you
can write your own in `app/Hooks/` or ship them via a dependency,
reference them by fully-qualified class name in this config, and
they get full access to `Repository`, `Config`, `Options`, and IO.

Typical custom actions teams add:
- Block commits to `main`/`master` directly
- Forbid `dd()` / `dump()` / `var_dump()` in staged files
- Assert every new PHP file has `declare(strict_types=1)`
- Require a migration file whenever a model changes
